One possibility worth ruling out is the checkpoint-id issue from #18098.

`DynamicCommitter` walks back through the branch history to the latest snapshot that has `flink.max-committed-checkpoint-id`, a matching job id, and a matching or absent operator id. It marks every request with a checkpoint id at or below that value as already committed. It does this whether or not the job was restored from state. So if a restart keeps the job id but starts the checkpoint counter again from a low value, new commits are skipped until the counter passes the old value. Records are consumed and checkpoints complete, the skipped requests don't count as pending, and the skip is only logged at DEBUG (`Skipping {} commit requests`). That would match what you see, but only if the restart actually reset the checkpoint counter. A normal recovery from a checkpoint doesn't do that.

#18123 fixed this for `IcebergSink`; the open #18303 proposes the same for `DynamicCommitter`.

